{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Sanctify to Me every firstborn, opener of every womb.</b> The verse should have said, 'Sanctify to Me every firstborn among the children of Israel, of man and beast,' since the plague of the firstborn struck both the firstborn of the father, who is the first of his strength, and the firstborn of the mother — as it is written, ״And HaShem struck every firstborn״ — and even the eldest of the household, if there is no firstborn there, for he too is called firstborn. But He added to write פטר כל רחם ('opener of every womb') because He did not wish to sanctify of the firstborns except the firstborn of the mother alone, for the firstborn of the father has uncertainty attached to it, unlike the firstborn of the mother. And also the firstborn of animals that were sanctified were firstborns of the mother, for thus it is written (Exodus 13:12): ״And you shall set apart every opener of womb to HaShem, and every opener of womb among cattle״, etc. Therefore we find that the prophet compared the love of the Holy One, blessed be He, for Israel to the love of a mother for her son, as he said (Isaiah 49:14–15): ״Can a woman forget her nursing child, the son of her womb?״ — that is, she will not show compassion to the son of her womb; and he did not say, 'Can a father forget his son.' And my master and teacher R. Solomon, of blessed memory, explained that this verse, ״Can a woman forget,״ comes to inform us of two things: the first, that there is no doubt concerning the people Israel that they are the children of the living God, just as there is no doubt concerning Reuben that he is his mother's son — this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 89:28): ״He shall call Me, You are my Father,״ etc., ״I also shall make him firstborn״; and the second, just as it is impossible for a mother to exchange her son for another, so it is impossible to exchange Israel for another nation. And He promised them: ״Even these may forget״ — that the deed of the calf, in which it is written ״These are your gods, O Israel,״ may be forgotten; ״but I will not forget you״ — that the deed of Sinai, in which it is written (Exodus 20), ״I am HaShem your Elohim,״ shall not be forgotten." "<b>And thus</b> they said in the Midrash: The plague of the firstborn in Egypt fell even upon the females, except for Bithiah daughter of Pharaoh, who was a firstborn and did not die, for she found a good advocate — this is Moses — as it is said (Exodus 2:2): ״And she saw him, that he was good.״ And concerning her Solomon said (Proverbs 31:18): ״She tastes that her merchandise is good; her lamp does not go out at night״ — that is, her soul; and that is the night of the plague of the firstborn."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:29.773194789Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}